{"conditionalSetIds":[],"forecasterId":"demographics-ai-2036:run1:growth:fermi","informationSetArtifactId":"sha256:607808a26d3f4a11a0742e195e947dbb4130b4870742ac5835e25913273b95f6","modelForecastIds":[],"modelUse":"none","prediction":{"kind":"quantiles","quantiles":[{"probability":0.1,"value":0.85},{"probability":0.5,"value":1.75},{"probability":0.9,"value":2.6}]},"questionHash":"sha256:e1b53adbdbe1bb1ccef339035b6376b49668b9341050c684f480d5b347e316f6","questionId":"demographics-ai-2036-cq3","questionVersion":"1.0.0","rationale":"PPP WEIGHTS, RECONCILED EXPLICITLY. Locked basis is constant 2025 USD PPP, so weights are FROZEN at 2025: China ~$42T PPP, OECD-38 ~$77T PPP, block ~$119T. China = 0.353, OECD-38 = 0.647. I FLAG the Outside-View Reference-Class Scout: he uses China 0.30 \"drifting to 0.32 by 2032-36\". Both the level and the drift are wrong under the lock — the level understates China by ~3-5 pts of weight, and any drift is chain-weighting, which constant-2025 weights forbid. His own headline drag term is consequently stated as -0.86 pp when his own inputs give -0.95 pp. The Inside-View Modeler's 0.36 and the Auditor's 0.35 are both acceptable.\nCHINA WEIGHT x CHINA DRAG, THE INTERACTION: -2.7 pp/yr x 0.353 = -0.953 pp on the block. The locked global aging drag is -1.1 pp/yr. So at coupled weights essentially the ENTIRE global aging drag arrives through China; the OECD-38's share of the global drag is ~0.15 pp. This single product is the largest term in the answer and it is near-deterministic because the cohorts already exist.\nOECD-38 BOTTOM-UP, 2032-36 (pp/yr):\n + population/headcount: +0.20 (migration-driven; US/CA/AU positive, JP/KR/IT negative)\n + locked per-capita baseline: +0.60 (already embeds the 31%->39-41% old-age-ratio drag)\n = baseline 0.80\n + capital deepening incl. the AI/grid capex stock: +0.12. Sizing matters and is routinely overstated: the incremental AI+grid capex above the 2015-22 norm runs ~1.2-1.5% of OECD GDP during 2026-2030, leaving ~5-6% of GDP of extra capital stock by the window. Against a K/Y of ~3.1 that is dK/K ~1.8% TOTAL, which at alpha=0.33 spread over five years is ~+0.12 pp/yr. A capex hump of the announced size arithmetically CANNOT contribute more than ~0.15 pp/yr to 2032-36 growth.\n + TFP, coupled to 2B: expected lift from AI-Q6 regimes = 0.162*0.75 + 0.472*0.35 + 0.365*0.05 = 0.122 + 0.165 + 0.018 = +0.305\n - Baumol/Jones reweighting: -0.11 (care+construction+grid share of OECD GVA 19%->23%, times a ~3 pp productivity gap; with task-bundle sigma ~0.4 the aggregate is pulled toward the slowest input as its share rises)\n + residual healthy-aging offset not already inside the locked 0.6%: +0.05 (the locked +0.4 pp/yr is GLOBAL and largely overlaps the baseline construction; counting it in full would double-count)\n - damages/frictions (climate, energy, trade fragmentation): -0.05\n + measurement wedge (capex booked as GFCF, impairments are balance-sheet not negative GDP, hedonic GPU/software deflators): +0.05\n = OECD-38 central 1.16%/yr, sigma 0.62\nCHINA BOTTOM-UP, 2032-36 (pp/yr): labour input -0.60 (working-age 15-59 falling ~1.2%/yr, beta ~0.5); capital deepening +1.60 (investment share ~38% decaying, K/Y ~3.5, alpha ~0.45); TFP +1.30 (robotics leadership plus residual catch-up at ~25% of US productivity, down from ~2.5% in the 2010s); property/local-government deleveraging and pension-subsidy crowding -0.30. TRUE sum = 2.00%. Add the NBS deflator/smoothing wedge of +0.50 to +0.70 (Nakamura-Steinsson-Liu and successors put it at 1-1.5 pp in boom years, less in slow years) because resolution uses the official print. MEASURED central 2.70%, sigma 1.30. THIS IS MY SHARPEST DISAGREEMENT WITH THE PANEL: all three Wave-1 panelists anchored China at 2.85-3.00% top-down (locked drag subtracted from a 6.5-7% trend). Bottom-up growth accounting cannot reach 2.9% without a TFP number China has not printed since 2015. The ~25 bp gap between my build and theirs IS the deflator wedge plus a generous capital-deepening assumption.\nBLOCK: 0.647*1.16 + 0.353*2.70 = 0.750 + 0.953 = 1.703 -> P50 1.75 after rounding for the shared measurement wedge.\nSPREAD: OECD contribution sigma = 0.647*0.62 = 0.40; China contribution sigma = 0.353*1.30 = 0.46; rho = +0.45 (common AI-capex and trade channels). Block sigma = sqrt(0.160 + 0.212 + 2*0.45*0.40*0.46) = sqrt(0.160+0.212+0.166) = sqrt(0.538) = 0.733. Symmetric 1.28-sigma band = 0.81/2.69. Applied skew: lower tail fattened (AI-Q4 at 66%, my coupled mark-up to ~72%, puts a digestion recession inside or immediately before the window, worth -60 to -100 bp on a 5-year average); upper tail truncated by the Baumol weakest link — reaching 2.6% requires OECD-38 above 2.0%, i.e. ~1.8%/yr per-capita, a rate no OECD-wide 5-year window has delivered outside 1995-2004 US.\nFINAL: P10 0.85 / P50 1.75 / P90 2.60.","referenceClassIds":[],"schemaVersion":"forecast-workbench.forecast/v1","sealedAt":"2026-11-04T17:31:54.799Z","seriesId":"published-panel","strongestContraryCase":"Not elicited per question. Panelist falsification signposts: {\"signpost\":\"G7 PPP-GDP-weighted 10y sovereign real yield (computed from the TIPS/linker route) prints a trailing 12-month average below 1.20% or above 2.90%, as read from FRED DFII10 plus the DE/UK/FR/IT/CA/JP linker curves.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31 2030\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Below 1.20%: my CQ4 P50 of 2.15 is wrong by more than one sigma and my whole right-skew is misplaced — shift CQ4 to roughly 1.25/1.55/2.40, move ~8 pts from CQ6-C into CQ6-D (the repression/demand-shortfall branch is realizing), and cut CQ5 conjunct A by ~10 pts because a bp-vol index deflates with the yield level. Above 2.90%: raise CQ4 to ~1.8/2.75/3.9, move ~6 pts from B into C, and raise CQ5 to ~36.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"OECD's published GDP-weighted (or PPP-GDP-weighted) OECD-38 core CPI aggregate, or the first OECD/IMF statistical note establishing which construction the market treats as canonical, shows Turkey/Mexico/Poland EXCLUDED or down-weighted toward their market-GDP shares.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31 2029\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"This is the single biggest falsifier of MY payload specifically, since my CQ2 of 42 and CQ6-C of 30 rest on a 0.60 weight to the literal all-38 construction. If the canonical object turns out advanced-economy-weighted, CQ2 falls from 42 to ~29 (conjunct A from 0.70 to ~0.50), CQ6-C falls from 30 to ~19, and CQ6-B rises to ~60. Conversely a published literal all-38 aggregate printing above 3.0% takes CQ2 to ~50 and CQ6-C to ~36.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"Cumulative announced-versus-impaired hyperscaler capex: either (i) >=20% of the ~$1.4T commitment stack publicly cancelled, deferred beyond 2032, or written down, or (ii) net external AI software/API revenue below 0.5x annualized AI compute D&A for two consecutive reported years.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31 2031\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Confirms AI-Q4 at my coupled 72% and is the main route into my CQ3 left tail. If observed BEFORE end-2029 (early bust): cut CQ3 P50 by ~20 bp to ~1.55, move ~6 pts from CQ6-B into CQ6-D, cut CQ4 P50 by ~25 bp (the +7 bp capex arrow dies and the -15 bp demand-shortfall arrow doubles), and cut CQ5 by ~5 pts because the modal bust is disinflationary and bonds rally. If NOT observed by end-2031 and announced capex is still being funded, raise CQ3 P50 to ~1.95 and CQ6-A from 9 to ~15.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"China official (NBS) annual real GDP growth prints below 3.0% for three consecutive years, or China headline CPI prints a 3-year average above 1.8%.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31 2032\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Sub-3.0% for three years confirms my bottom-up build against the panel's 2.85-3.00% top-down anchor: hold CQ3 P50 at 1.75 or cut to 1.65, raise CQ6-D from 10 to ~14 and cut CQ6-A to ~7. A 3-year China CPI average above 1.8% breaks CQ2's conjunct B (0.66 marginal): CQ2 falls from 42 to roughly 0.70*0.30 = 21, and CQ6-C loses ~4 pts because the bifurcation that de